frankensim.bar-card.v1
# Marimba Gb2 over La Favre's F#2 tube, its stop set to sit the tube on this bar; its loss Hill's printed whole-bar loss factor in place of the three-bar fit; its elastic ratios the rosewood Beaton and Scavone (2021) print from Bork et al. (1999), its undercut solved again on them; everything else as the Gb2 tube card.
name	marimba-gb2-hill-tubetuned-rosewood-rubber
source	Bar outline: J. La Favre, lafavre.us/marimba-bars.htm, table of an unnamed commercial 5-octave marimba with rosewood bars: F# row, length 19 5/8 in; width 2 1/2 in and thickness 0.92 in from the G row. Tube: the same page, La Favre's own padouk marimba, F#2 resonator: aluminium tubing, outer diameter 3 1/2 in, wall 1/16 in, air column not measured, total resonator length 38 9/16 in at the tube's centre, mitered in 3 sections, 45 degrees at each joint, 1 in below the bottom of the bar. Wood: A. Chaigne and V. Doutaut, Numerical simulations of xylophones. I, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 101(1), 539-557 (1997), Table I p.544, Rosewood bar: E 2.13e10, density 1015, eta 2.35e-7 s, gamma_B 51.58 1/s, through the packs named below (E and density are read from the pack of Doutaut and Chaigne 1994, which prints the same values). Ratios: D. Beaton and G. Scavone, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 149(6), 3758 (2021), Table II p.3760, rosewood "adopted from the literature (Bork et al., 1999)", a C3 marimba bar: E 23.0, 2.3 and 1.15 GPa, G 3.0, 1.0 and 3.0 GPa (xy, yz, xz) and nu 0.30, 0.60 and 0.45 (xy, yz, xz), x along the bar, y across it and z through it; only the eight ratios to E_x are read, second hand, since Bork et al. (1999) is unread. Mallet: the same Table I, Korogi No. 4 rubber, K 3.7e7, 23.6 g, measured on a padouk bar; 1.0 m/s and strike 15.9 cm of 29.3 cm as the paper's Fig. 8.
note	NOT in the source: the tube's stop. La Favre prints no air column for the F#2 tube ("not measured"), only its total length of 38 9/16 in, so this card sets the stop as a maker would, at 897.368 mm (35.33 in), solved once on the passive column with the bar's reflection in it for the cut before these ratios; on this bar it leaves the tube's first resonance +0.035 c from this bar's f1 of 92.499 Hz, under the 0.1 c that would set it again, and the law here reads 897.386 mm; the tube's cross-section and gap are as printed and no recording enters. La Favre set each stopper by ear and says the figures aren't absolute. La Favre's total length leaves 3.23 in (82.1 mm) below this stop, for the stopper assembly and tuning up to 85 F; the assembly's thickness isn't printed, so whether this stop can reach isn't known. NOT in the source either: the undercut (solved here in the engine on this card's ratios, modes only, a circular arc, half reach 249.188 mm, the blank's length limit, and middle 5.492 mm, solved alone so f1 sits at equal temperament; f2/f1 came out 4.015, untargeted, where the builder's statement for its own padouk bars is 4 from A3 to C5; the rosewood table prints no arch length and no tuning). Strike fraction carried from the xylophone bar; no marimba strike is printed. Not the recording's bar or mallet: Iowa states no bar wood and no bar outline; the recording's file names a cord mallet or a roll, and a Korogi rubber xylophone mallet is not that mallet. Tube and bar come from two instruments: the bar from the unnamed commercial table, the tube from La Favre's own marimba, printed at A4 = 442 and 72 F, rendered here at A4 = 440 in the card's gas; no correction made. "Bottom of bar" is read as the plane of the uncut lower face, so the wood at the bar's middle is a further 17.9 mm from the mouth. The tube is a closed-open column of eight odd modes, every one under this card's band that the open-end law takes (ka under 1.5), coupled through the bar's own boundary elements at each tube mode's frequency; its wall doesn't scatter, the mouth's sound doesn't reflect off the bar on its way out, and there are no neighbouring bars or frame. La Favre's F#2 tube is mitered in 3 sections, 45 degrees at each joint; this one is straight, with the same cross-section. Its mouth, 85.7 mm across inside, is wider than this 63.5 mm bar. The loss is one printed whole-bar loss factor, eta 0.0045 at every mode, the mean as printed (the printed range, 0.0036 to 0.0068, is its limit), from H. Hill's unrefereed 2011 student report (NSF REU, University of Illinois Physics 406): the bars of a Yamaha YM-5100A, Honduras rosewood per the maker's spec for that model, recorded in a room with the resonators off, so eta holds the wood, the cord and the bar's own radiation, and this card's tube adds only its own losses. The stiffness and density stay the rosewood rows cited above, so the loss and the stiffness come from different rosewoods. It was tried after seeing that the pages decay too fast on the three-bar fit, with no recording number in it; Hill's bars run C2 to C7, so f1 of 92.5 Hz lies inside them. The modes are certified at a relative half-width of 1e-6, the most a card allows, because for the cut before these ratios origin's mode certificate refused one window at 1e-7, and this bar was not run at 1e-7; the largest half-width is 7.0e-6 Hz, on the mode at 826.671 Hz. Nothing above 1460 Hz is in this render: the band stays at the solve's window plan.
